AUDIT-047,Patient-controlled analgesia. Does a concurrent opioid infusion improve pain management after surgery?,"Based on the context provided, the answer to the question ""Does a concurrent opioid infusion improve pain management after surgery?"" is as follows:

The context indicates that several studies have looked at the use of patient-controlled analgesia (PCA) systems with a concurrent opioid (morphine) infusion for pain management after surgery:

1. The first piece of context mentions a study to ""assess the influence of a continuous (basal) morphine infusion as part of a patient-controlled analgesia (PCA) system on the postoperative analgesic requirement and on recovery parameters following abdominal hysterectomy."" This suggests that the study is evaluating whether the concurrent morphine infusion as part of the PCA system can improve pain management after this type of surgery.

2. Another piece of context states that the researchers ""asked whether intrathecal morphine or periarticular multimodal drug injection provides better pain control with fewer adverse events."" This suggests they are comparing different methods of opioid administration, including concurrent infusion, to determine which provides better pain control.

3. The last piece of context compares the efficacy of IV PCA with morphine combined with continuous fentanyl administration versus placebo fentanyl patches for pain management after major orthopedic procedures. This also indicates an evaluation of concurrent opioid infusion as part of the PCA regimen.

Overall, the context provided indicates that the studies are investigating whether a concurrent opioid infusion as part of a PCA system can improve pain management and recovery after various types of surgery, suggesting that this approach may be beneficial for managing postoperative pain.","[1] Prospective double-blind randomized study evaluating pain intensity after surgery under general anesthesia.

[2] --To assess the influence of a continuous (basal) morphine infusion as part of a patient-controlled analgesia (PCA) system on the postoperative analgesic requirement and on recovery parameters following abdominal hysterectomy.

[3] We asked whether intrathecal morphine or periarticular multimodal drug injection provides better pain control with fewer adverse events.

[4] Whether intraoperative analgesics have an impact on postoperative cancer recurrence is unknown. Some investigations suggest that the opioids could favor relapse and that regional analgesia and nonsteroidal antiinflammatory drugs could improve cancer prognosis. We retrospectively reviewed our series of breast cancer surgery patients.

[5] To compare, in patients who underwent major orthopedic surgical procedures, the efficacy of intravenous (IV) patient-controlled analgesia (PCA) with morphine combined with continuous administration of two doses of fentanyl or placebo via transdermal therapeutic system with fentanyl (TTSF) patches.",,,,
