AUDIT-064,Length of stay and short-term functional outcomes after total knee arthroplasty: Can we predict them?,"Based on the given context, it appears that the research aims to identify variables that influence length of stay (LOS) and short-term functional outcomes in patients undergoing total knee arthroplasty (TKA). The key points are:

1. The studies aimed to identify variables that influence LOS and short-term functional outcomes after TKA.

2. Some of the variables examined include the perceived technical quality of the surgery and the effect of different TKA implant designs on clinical and functional outcomes.

3. The studies also looked at the impact of rapid recovery protocols, where patients are discharged on the day of surgery or on postoperative day 1, on 30-day complication and readmission rates.

4. Another aim was to examine whether function and pain outcomes of patients undergoing primary TKA are changing over time.

In summary, the research suggests that it is possible to predict LOS and short-term functional outcomes after TKA by examining various factors, such as surgical technique, implant design, and recovery protocols. The studies indicate that these variables can influence the clinical and functional outcomes of patients undergoing TKA.","[1] We asked whether a surgeon can predict postoperative Knee Society scores (KSSs) of TKAs at the time of surgery based on perceived technical quality of surgery.

[2] To identify variables influencing length of stay (LOS) and short-term functional outcome in patients undergoing total knee arthroplasty (TKA). A secondary aim was to verify the effect of the same variables on blood management and the rate of postoperative infection.

[3] To examine whether function and pain outcomes of patients undergoing primary total knee arthroplasty (TKA) are changing over time.

[4] Length of hospital stay is a quality metric in joint arthroplasty. Rapid recovery protocols have safely reduced the average length of hospitalization, but it is unclear whether there is a difference in complication and readmission rates between patients discharged the day of surgery or on postoperative day 1 (POD 1). We calculated 30-day complication and readmission after total knee arthroplasty (TKA), total hip arthroplasty (THA), and unicompartmental knee arthroplasty (UKA) based on day of discharge. We then analyzed the rapid recovery group by comparing those discharged the day of surgery and those discharged on POD 1.

