AUDIT-084,Does a DVD improve compliance with home exercise programs for people who have sustained a traumatic hand injury?,"Based on the context provided, the answer to the question ""Does a DVD improve compliance with home exercise programs for people who have sustained a traumatic hand injury?"" is:

No, the study did not find a significant improvement in the mean exercise compliance score between the group that received a DVD and brochures compared to the group that received only brochures. The context states that ""No significant improvement was found in the mean exercise compliance score (p = 0.344) between the intervention and control groups."" Therefore, the provision of a DVD along with brochures did not improve compliance with the home exercise program in this study.

The key findings from the context are:
- Patients with traumatic hand injuries were randomly assigned to either receive a DVD and brochures (intervention group) or just brochures (control group).
- Compliance was measured through exercise diaries, clinic attendance, exercise checklists, and a follow-up survey.
- The study found no significant difference in the mean exercise compliance score between the two groups.
- Factors like pain and lack of time were reported by participants as limiting their ability to perform the home exercises.

So, based on the information provided, the use of a DVD did not improve compliance with the home exercise program compared to using brochures alone for this population with traumatic hand injuries.","[1] This feasibility study sought to determine if compliance and understanding of a home exercise program following a traumatic hand injury is improved when patients are provided with a DVD and a brochure when compared to using brochures only.

[2] Patients who presented with a traumatic hand injury and commenced on a hand therapy protocol were randomly assigned into two groups. The control group received brochures while the experimental group were provided with exercise instructions on DVD as well as brochures. Compliance was measured through the use of exercise diaries, clinic attendance, a checklist to measure correctness and understanding of exercises and a follow-up survey.

[3] To determine whether a repetitive training of complex movements of arm and hand contributes to functional recovery in stroke patients.

[4] No significant improvement was found in the mean exercise compliance score (p = 0.344) between the intervention and control groups. From the survey results almost half of all participants reported that pain interfered in their ability to perform their home exercises and a third acknowledged that time limited their ability to perform their exercises.

